Chapter: GopKey

 Scancode source for Option A payloads running on the H1a runtime
 spine. runtime-init installs the IDT and unmasks the keyboard IRQ,
 so scancodes arrive in the kernel key-buffer cell (28680) from our
 own interrupt handler. Polling data port 0x60 directly would race
 that handler for the byte and lose it. gk-take consumes the cell
 read-and-clear: zero means no key is waiting. codex-vm delivers
 window and -keys scancodes through the same IRQ path once the guest
 has programmed the PIC, so cell consumption behaves identically
 under the emulator, under OVMF, and on metal.

 The cell is a mailbox, not a queue, and that is deliberate. A key
 struck while the machine is busy -- generating a keypair, hashing the
 seed -- is dropped rather than replayed into whatever screen appears
 next. Buffering type-ahead across a screen boundary would let a stray
 keystroke pre-fill a passphrase field, or a stray Enter confirm a
 verdict nobody read. During a prompt the consumer polls in a
 constant-stack loop that allocates nothing, so it never falls behind
 a human hand.

 The read and the clear are one instruction. atomic-exchange compiles
 to XCHG against memory, which asserts LOCK on x86 whether or not the
 prefix is written, so the keyboard ISR cannot land between the read
 and the clear and have its scancode zeroed. A plain peek-then-poke
 loses a key in that window. The window is two instructions wide and
 no test would ever catch it.

 The exchange is 64 bits and the ISR stores the scancode zero-extended
 to 64, so the low byte is the whole answer; masking keeps gk-take
 total no matter who else has written the cell.

Section: Key Cell

  gk-cell : Integer = 28680

  gk-take : Integer -> Integer
  gk-take (dummy) = bit-and (atomic-exchange gk-cell 0) 255
